From: Gaia Image Builder <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] linux: build the dtc parser with the machine's bison and flex when new enough

With a device tree kernel, linux/linux.mk added host-bison and host-flex to
the kernel's dependencies unconditionally: the kernel generates its dtc
parser at build time, and the comment said Buildroot's own were used "for
reproducibility". host-bison needs host-m4, so this cost a long host stage
on every machine (measured: bison 132.7 s and flex 39.2 s in one build).

The kernel now depends on $(BR2_BISON_HOST_DEPENDENCY) and
$(BR2_FLEX_HOST_DEPENDENCY), which are empty for a machine program that is
at least Buildroot's own bison 3.8.2 and flex 2.6.4 (see 0003), and are
host-bison and host-flex otherwise. The kernel's dtc binary, and the dtbs
it compiles, are the same whichever parser generator made the parser; that
is checked for one kernel tree with a bison from each.

---
 linux/linux.mk | 7 ++++---
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/linux/linux.mk b/linux/linux.mk
--- a/linux/linux.mk
+++ b/linux/linux.mk
@@ -479,9 +479,10 @@
 
 ifeq ($(BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DTS_SUPPORT),y)
 # Starting with 4.17, the generated dtc parser code is no longer
-# shipped with the kernel sources, so we need flex and bison. For
-# reproducibility, we use our owns rather than the host ones.
-LINUX_DEPENDENCIES += host-bison host-flex
+# shipped with the kernel sources, so we need flex and bison. The
+# machine's ones are used when they are new enough, and host-bison and
+# host-flex are built otherwise (check-host-bison-flex.mk).
+LINUX_DEPENDENCIES += $(BR2_BISON_HOST_DEPENDENCY) $(BR2_FLEX_HOST_DEPENDENCY)
 
 ifeq ($(BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DTB_IS_SELF_BUILT),)
 define LINUX_BUILD_DTB
